---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import PageIndex from '../components/PageIndex.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';
import { href, sourceUrl } from '../lib/constants.mjs';
---
<SiteLayout title="Workflows — Nora SuperIntelligence Lab" description="How the AI Coordinator connects knowledge, role-based evaluation and two owner-review loops." path="/workflows/">
  <article class="container content-page" data-supporting-page="workflows">
    <h1>Workflows</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">The AI Coordinator plans work, chooses tools and model routes, delegates focused tasks and combines the results. Three workflows share that coordination: question answering, ingestion, and artifact evaluation and revision.</p>
    <PageIndex />
    <nav class="workflow-map" aria-label="Choose a workflow">
      <a href={href('/walkthroughs/?flow=request')}><strong>Question answering</strong><span>Retrieve relevant evidence, synthesize an answer and retain the conversation.</span></a>
      <a href={href('/walkthroughs/?flow=ingestion')}><strong>Ingestion</strong><span>Prepare and verify a knowledge release before making it available to retrieval.</span></a>
      <a href={href('#artifact-review')}><strong>Artifact evaluation and revision</strong><span>Collect judge feedback, revise against owner criteria and obtain final human approval.</span></a>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="harness" data-index-label="Shared coordination">One coordinator, three workflows</h2>
    <p>Task orchestration determines what work to do and how to combine it. Model routing chooses a backend for a particular model call. Ingestion runs outside conversations, so document processing does not become part of every user request.</p>
    <div class="workflow-diagram"><Diagram showTitle={false} title="Harness workflows" previewSrc="/diagrams/harness-workflows-preview.svg" htmlUrl="/diagrams/harness-workflows.html" alt="Question answering, ingestion and artifact evaluation share the harness and its knowledge services." /></div>

    <h2 id="judge-registry" data-index-label="Judge registry">Prepare judges before running a review</h2>
    <p>Research into the role, domain and relevant sources informs each judge definition: its perspective, rubric, criteria, dataset and examples. Version those definitions separately from the artifact being reviewed.</p>
    <p>Prepared Harbor tasks and datasets form the <strong>NIS Judge Registry</strong>. Select the roles a task needs: CTO, AI Architect, Hiring Manager, Senior Engineer, Domain Expert or Synthetic Owner. These are synthetic review perspectives; several roles using one model can share the same blind spots.</p>
    <div class="workflow-diagram"><Diagram showTitle={false} title="NIS Judge Registry" previewSrc="/diagrams/judge-registry-preview.svg" htmlUrl="/diagrams/judge-registry.html" alt="Research informs versioned role judge definitions and prepared Harbor tasks and datasets." /></div>
    <p>The Synthetic Owner definition draws on sanitized analysis of relevant owner interactions and preferences. It gives a recommendation; it does not replace the person’s final decision.</p>

    <h2 id="artifact-review" data-index-label="Evaluate and revise">Two review loops, one final owner</h2>
    <p>Freeze the artifact, source evidence, task brief and judge versions. The AI Coordinator selects the relevant judges, dispatches evaluations and aggregates the returned findings while retaining each role’s evidence and reasoning.</p>
    <div class="workflow-diagram"><Diagram showTitle={false} title="Artifact evaluation and feedback" previewSrc="/diagrams/artifact-review-flow-preview.svg" htmlUrl="/diagrams/artifact-review-flow.html" alt="The AI Coordinator collects Harbor Hub and Hugging Face feedback. Synthetic Owner scoring drives an inner revision loop; Real Owner approval or changes drive the outer loop." /></div>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Synthetic Owner review:</strong> score the artifact and recommend accept or reject. Below the configured threshold, revise the artifact and brief, then evaluate again.</li>
      <li><strong>Real Owner review:</strong> bring qualifying work and its feedback to the person. Approval completes the review; requested changes return the artifact through revision and evaluation.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Harbor Hub</strong> provides the prepared-task and results route. <strong>Hugging Face</strong> provides judge-model access through a separate coordinator route. There is no Harbor-to-Hugging Face dependency, and Hugging Face does not hold the judge registry.</p>
    <p>Carry useful lessons into the next brief and judge definitions. Preserve their versions so scores remain comparable. This feedback loop updates the work and its review criteria; it does not automatically train model weights. A higher score alone is not evidence of a better artifact.</p>

    <h2 id="example" data-index-label="Atlas example">Atlas: a concrete revision</h2>
    <p>The fictional Atlas plan requires a passing load test and a completed rollback rehearsal. A draft briefing mentions only the load test.</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>“Add the rollback rehearsal.”</dt><dd>Accept: it is an explicit source requirement. Add it to the next version.</dd>
      <dt>“Promise release will take five minutes.”</dt><dd>Reject: the source provides no duration. Keep the timing unknown.</dd>
      <dt>“Add an open-questions section.”</dt><dd>If the current version already has one, mark the suggestion already resolved.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p><strong>Retrieval quality</strong> asks whether search and read found the right evidence. <strong>Artifact quality</strong> asks whether the output uses that evidence accurately and serves the task. Keep those evaluations separate.</p>
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('docs/workflows/artifact-review/README.md', 'Local example')}>saved-review example</a> replays captured feedback and records explicit decisions without making model calls. <a href={href('/validation/')}>Validation</a> separates those local contracts from live execution evidence. Explore the <a href={href('/architecture/')}>architecture</a>, follow a <a href={href('/walkthroughs/')}>knowledge walkthrough</a>, or read the <a href={sourceUrl('docs/workflows/artifact-review/README.md')}>artifact-review guide in the public Harness</a>.</p>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
<style>
  .content-page { padding-top: 40px; padding-bottom: 64px; min-width: 0; }
  p, li, dd { font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.7; max-width: 66ch; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .workflow-map { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(230px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; margin: 32px 0 40px; }
  .workflow-map a { display: grid; align-content: start; gap: 8px; min-height: 128px; padding: 20px; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 16px; text-decoration: none; }
  .workflow-map a:hover { border-color: var(--teal-light); }
  .workflow-map strong { color: var(--ink); font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .workflow-map span { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  h2 { margin-top: 2.5em; scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--header-height) + 24px); }
  dt { margin-top: 1.2rem; font-weight: 700; }
  dd { margin: .35rem 0 0; }
  .workflow-diagram { margin: 28px 0; min-width: 0; }
</style>
